# {{ ansible_managed }}
# =============================================================================
# Curated alarm — undervoltage (ADR-030, phase 2)
# =============================================================================
# Replaces the rpi_volt check in homelab-health.sh, once seen to fire. Until
# then both run — and this one matters more than most: undervoltage is the
# classic silent Pi killer, and the most likely cause behind an "unexplained
# poweroff", which the runbook answers with an SD reflash.
#
# The firmware exposes an alarm BIT rather than a voltage, which is why
# `sensors` shows `in0: N/A` and why an earlier attempt concluded this could not
# be migrated at all. It can: Netdata surfaces the bit as a chart with `clear`,
# `critical` and `fault` dimensions — verified reading clear=1 critical=0
# fault=0.
#
# `critical` is the dimension that corresponds to what the script reads at
# /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*/in0_lcrit_alarm.
#
# The `lookup` window is what makes this alarm work, and the reason written here
# until 2026-09-19 was wrong. It said the bit "is latched: once set it stays set
# until cleared". It is not: raspberrypi-hwmon.c asks the firmware to clear the
# sticky bits on every poll and polls every 2 s, so the bit is a pulse at most
# 2 s wide. What saves this alarm is that the chart's update_every is 1 and the
# lookup takes `max` over a whole minute, so a 2 s pulse is sampled with
# probability ~1 and survives until the alarm next evaluates. An instantaneous
# read at a one-minute cadence would have had a 3% chance of seeing the event.
# Do not lengthen the collection interval on the strength of the old sentence.
#
# No hysteresis and no `up` delay: waiting to report an electrical fault would
# be the wrong trade. `down 15m` is what holds the verdict long enough to
# outlive the pulse and reach the Kuma adapter, which runs every 5 minutes.
#
# `units: events` rather than `status`, because the units travel into the Kuma
# message and from there into a Discord notification. "undervoltage 0 status"
# says nothing to someone reading it cold; "undervoltage 0 events" does.
# =============================================================================

 template: homelab_undervoltage
       on: system.hw.sensor.voltage.alarm
    class: Errors
     type: System
component: Power supply
   lookup: max -1m unaligned of critical
    units: events
    every: 1m
     warn: $this > 0
    delay: up 0 down 15m
  summary: Homelab undervoltage reported by the firmware
     info: The firmware reported an undervoltage event — check the PSU and cable
       to: silent
